**(i) Inlier.** An **inlier** is an area (outcrop) of **older rocks completely surrounded by younger rocks**. It is formed when **erosion removes the younger covering rocks** and exposes the older rocks beneath, in one of these ways:

- the crest of an **anticline (or dome)** is eroded down through the younger beds, so the older core is exposed, surrounded by younger beds dipping away from it;
- the **upthrown block of a fault** (or a horst) is eroded;
- a **river valley** cuts down through horizontal younger rocks (for example through an unconformity) into older rocks;
- in a thrust area, erosion cuts through a **thrust sheet (nappe)** to expose the older rocks of the lower sheet, giving a **window (fenster)**.

On a map the outcrop of the older rock lies inside the younger outcrop, with the beds dipping away from the older rock (in the case of an anticline or dome).

**(ii) Dome.** A **dome** is a **circular or oval upfold** in which the **beds dip outward in all directions (away from a central point)**. It is formed by **vertical upward force** from below, for example:

- the **intrusion of magma** (laccolith or batholith) or of **salt (a salt dome or diapir)**, which pushes the overlying strata up;
- **uplift** of the crust, or the crossing of two sets of folds (anticlines) at right angles.

When a dome is **eroded**, the **oldest rock is found at the centre**, with younger rocks forming concentric rings (a circular or oval outcrop pattern) around it. A dome that has been eroded to its core is also an inlier. Domes can trap **oil and gas** (an anticlinal trap) when covered by an impermeable cap rock.

*Sketches to draw:* a block diagram of a dome with the dip arrows pointing outward, and its map pattern (oldest rock in the middle, concentric younger rings); an inlier surrounded by younger beds on a simple geological map.
